WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.477
MERGE is an optional advanced topic in this module.

00:00:03.477 --> 00:00:07.182
It can synchronize a source dataset with a target table.

00:00:07.182 --> 00:00:13.367
If a source record matches an existing target record, one action such as UPDATE can run.

00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:17.942
If there is no match, another action such as INSERT can run.

00:00:17.942 --> 00:00:24.722
The most important part is the matching condition because it defines what PostgreSQL considers the same

00:00:24.722 --> 00:00:25.152
record.

00:00:25.152 --> 00:00:32.252
If your browser engine supports the example, run it and inspect how matched and unmatched rows are handled.

00:00:32.252 --> 00:00:39.387
Do not worry if MERGE feels more complex than the earlier DML statements.

00:00:39.387 --> 00:00:42.387
The core skills are still SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, and safe matching.

00:00:42.387 --> 00:00:50.342
Before using MERGE in important workflows, check for duplicate source keys and verify the match logic very

00:00:50.342 --> 00:00:56.867
carefully. Connect this idea to a data engineer moving semi-structured API payloads into PostgreSQL.

00:00:56.867 --> 00:01:02.627
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:01:02.627 --> 00:01:10.367
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:16.122
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:16.122 --> 00:01:21.819
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
